'''Mictlán''' is the third planet from the star [[Aurelius]], orbiting at a mean distance of 0.62 AU between the super-greenhouse world [[Ciguapa]] and the gas giant [[Quetzal]]. Roughly equal in size and mass to [[Adonia]] (0.95 $M_\text{Adonia}$), it is a world of dramatic physical duality defined by its 1:1 synchronous tidal locking. One hemisphere of Mictlán permanently confronts the blinding fury of Aurelius, while the opposite hemisphere faces the perpetual cold of interstellar space. This tidally enforced dichotomy produces a planetary landscape divided into two extremes: a dayside of churning molten rock and a nightside of frozen volatile glaciers.<ref name="Yomi_Synthesis">Okaiken Institute for Space and Aeronautical Science (OISAS). (2019). *Thermal Topography and Atmospheric Circulation of the Tidally Locked Planet Mictlán: Results from the Yomi-3 Orbiter*. OISAS Planetary Science Series, 58, 22–65.</ref>

At the substellar point—where Aurelius hangs eternally at zenith—lies an immense, churning expanse of liquid silicate lava known as ''The Cauldron'' (Insulonian: ''El Caldero'', Sanese: ''Yomi no Kama'' / 黄泉の釜). Radiating at temperatures above 2,150 K (1,880 °C), this molten ocean continuously boils off rock vapor into a specialized mineral atmosphere composed of vaporized silicon monoxide, sodium, iron, and magnesium. These mineral vapors are swept toward the limb by ferocious supersonic winds, condensing into high-altitude clouds of liquid rock and quartz crystals that rain molten enstatite over the twilight frontier.<ref name="Aldana_Mictlan">Aldana, E. (2023). *Mineral Atmospheres and Silicate Precipitation Cycles on Ultra-Hot Synchronous Worlds*. Castejón Astrophysical Review, 88(1), 45–74.</ref>

In contrast, the nightside plunges to a frigid 60 K (−213 °C), forming an eternal volatile cold-trap where atmospheric nitrogen, carbon dioxide, and methane freeze out into continent-scale ice sheets. Between these two realms lies the narrow, tempestuous twilight belt—a band of perpetual twilight swept by abrasive mineral sandstorms and high-speed katabatic jet streams.<ref name="Yomi_Synthesis" />

==Physical characteristics==

===The Dayside and The Cauldron===
The sunward hemisphere receives a steady stellar irradiance of approximately 4,450 W/m²—over 2.6 times the flux reaching Adonia. At the substellar point, the crust has completely melted into a permanent, convective magma sea measuring approximately 2,400 kilometers in diameter and up to 45 kilometers deep, known as ''The Cauldron''.<ref name="Yomi_Synthesis" />

The surface of The Cauldron is a churning, incandescent expanse of ultra-mafic basaltic and komatiitic lava, radiating in vibrant shades of incandescent yellow, orange, and dull red. Convective plumes carry superheated magma from the mantle to the surface at velocities of several meters per second, where it boils vigorously. Around the margins of the magma sea lies a fractured, semi-plastic "sponge zone" of cooling black volcanic scoria, intersected by incandescent rifts and active volcanic caldera chains.<ref name="Aldana_Mictlan" />

===Atmosphere and mineral rain===
Mictlán possesses an active, dual-layer atmosphere with a mean surface pressure of approximately 150 kPa (1.5 atmospheres).<ref name="Yomi_Synthesis" /> The lower dayside atmosphere is composed predominantly of rock vapor, dominated by silicon monoxide ($SiO$), atomic sodium and potassium, monatomic iron and magnesium, molecular nitrogen, and trace carbon monoxide.<ref name="Aldana_Mictlan" />

As stellar heating drives intense thermal expansion at the substellar point, buoyant rock vapor ascends to altitudes of 35–50 km and flows rapidly toward the nightside along upper-level pressure gradients. As the mineral vapor approaches the cooler limb, temperatures drop below the condensation points of silicates (approx. 1,600 K). Silicon and magnesium oxides condense into microscopic enstatite and forsterite crystals, forming shimmering lavender and golden-tinted clouds. These clouds unleash showers of liquid silicate raindrops—literal molten rock rain—which solidify into glassy gravel and pelletal lapilli before striking the jagged basaltic plains of the terminator.<ref name="Aldana_Mictlan" />

===The Twilight Belt (The Terminator)===
The boundary between day and night is marked by a 400-kilometer-wide twilight strip where Aurelius appears permanently bisected by the horizon. The colossal temperature differential between the dayside ($> 2,000$ K) and the nightside ($< 70$ K) drives an extreme planetary heat engine. Dense, freezing nightside air plunges toward the dayside along the surface as ferocious katabatic winds reaching velocities of 800 km/h (Mach 0.7 in the dense air).<ref name="Yomi_Synthesis" />

These howling terminator winds pick up fine silicate dust, obsidian shards, and metallic iron particles, creating global sandstorms of immense abrasive power that have planed down ancient impact craters into jagged, knife-edged yardangs and fluted canyons. Temperatures across the twilight zone transition precipitously from 800 K to 150 K within a distance of less than 200 kilometers.<ref name="Aldana_Mictlan" />

===The Nightside and volatile ice sheets===
The dark hemisphere has been shrouded in night for hundreds of millions of years. Without internal heat conduction capable of overcoming the absence of solar flux, surface temperatures fall to 58–75 K (−215 to −198 °C).<ref name="Yomi_Synthesis" />

Because the nightside acts as a cold trap, volatile gases that circulate from the dayside are permanently frozen out onto the terrain. The nightside is covered in the vast ''Cocytus Ice Sheet''—a massive, undulating glacier of solid nitrogen ($N_2$), methane ($CH_4$), and carbon dioxide ($CO_2$) up to four kilometers thick, resting upon a frozen bed of water-ice bedrock. Geothermal vents and residual mantle plumes occasionally breach the ice sheet, generating spectacular cryo-volcanic geysers that vent pressurized nitrogen gas and dark organic tholins tens of kilometers into the night sky.<ref name="Yomi_Synthesis" />

==Orbit and rotation==

Mictlán orbits Aurelius at a mean distance of 0.62 AU, completing one orbital revolution every 181.0 standard days in a slightly eccentric orbit ($e = 0.090$).

Due to strong tidal torques exerted by Aurelius over its 4.6-billion-year lifespan, Mictlán's axial rotation has been completely synchronized with its orbital period (1:1 tidal lock). Its sidereal rotation period is exactly equal to its orbital period of 181.0 standard days. Because of its slight orbital eccentricity ($e = 0.090$), the planet experiences a small longitudinal libration of approximately 10.3 degrees. As a consequence, an observer situated within the twilight belt would witness Aurelius oscillate gently above and below the horizon over the course of a 181-day cycle, creating a periodic "twilight sunrise and sunset" that modulates local katabatic wind speeds.<ref name="Aldana_Dynamics">Aldana, E., & Thorne, A. (2022). ''Tidal Dissipation and Rotational Dynamics in Eccentric Terrestrial Systems''. Castejón Astrophysical Review, 44(2), 112–138.</ref>

Mictlán's orbit is gravitationally stabilized by its outer neighbor, the gas giant [[Quetzal]] (0.862 AU), whose periodic gravitational sweeps prevent orbital eccentricities from growing chaotically.<ref name="Aldana_Dynamics" />

==Observation and exploration==

In ancient mythology, Mictlán's reddish, flickering appearance and its tight orbit made it a symbol of doom, war, and the subterranean underworld. In ancient Insulonian legend, it was named after Mictlán, the nine-layered resting place of the dead, ruled by the lord of shadows and bone.<ref name="Navassa_Lex">Navassa Society of Historical Linguistics. (2018). ''Classical Astronomical Nomenclature of the Insulonian Basin''. Port d'Espens University Press.</ref> In Sanese astronomical records, it was cataloged as '''Yomisei''' ({{nihongo||黄泉星||"underworld star"}}), associated with the mythical realm of Yomi and considered an ominous omen when appearing in close conjunction with [[Quetzal]].<ref name="Tanigaki_Hist">Tanigaki, R., & Vives, M. (2012). ''Sun and Stars in the History of Human Thought: From Archaic Gnomons to Spaceborne Coronagraphy''. University of Okami Press.</ref>

Scientific exploration of Mictlán began in the late 20th century. In 1998, the automated probe *Yomi-1* launched by the Okaiken Space Agency performed the first multi-spectral flyby of the planet, confirming the existence of The Cauldron and the mineral vapor atmosphere.<ref name="Yomi_Synthesis" /> In 2016, the *Yomi-3 Orbiter* successfully inserted into a polar orbit around Mictlán, providing continuous thermal infrared mapping of the magma ocean, tracking supersonic terminator dust storms, and radar-sounding the depth of the nightside nitrogen ice sheets.<ref name="Yomi_Synthesis" />

==Name and etymology==

The name ''Mictlán'' originates from ancient Meso-Insulonian cosmology, referring to the vast, subterranean underworld of the dead. The name was officially adopted by the [[Balisca|Baliscan Royal Astronomical Society]] in 1882 to reflect the planet's infernal, half-molten and half-frozen landscape.<ref name="Navassa_Lex" />

In [[Sanese language|Sanese]], the planet has been known since the Sengoku astronomical chronicles as '''Yomisei''' ({{nihongo||黄泉星||"star of the underworld"}}), maintaining the identical mythological thematic resonance of eternal shadow and subterranean fire.<ref name="Tanigaki_Hist" />

